## TokenLighten MCP (medium)

Root `AGENTS.md` owns active designs/plan. Edit canonical templates, regenerate AI rules; never edit generated blocks.

TL returns exact slices + edit handles cheaply. Start EVERY file task with TL before native reads or Explore subagents: `read_file {query:"<verbatim request>",task:{epoch:"new"}}` with known `targets`. Native only after non-complete scope/verified absence, or if TL is unavailable/host reports native-bypass; never activate TL mid-task. Every call: own `cwd`; shared workspace: fixed `lane`. `task.profile`: `answer`=read-only, `generic`=change; omit if unknown, but declare it whenever the task kind is already known. Same-epoch requirements persist; a narrowed re-pack may be incomplete.

<!-- tl-legacy-compat:start -->
Legacy v0.14 input (`mode`/`paths`/`handles`/`maxBytes`/`maxTokens`/`taskProfile`/`taskEpoch`) needs TL_LEGACY_INPUT=accept (RC=refuse); never emit it — `next` is canonical.
<!-- tl-legacy-compat:end -->

Every reply is `{v:1,kind,...}`; branch on `kind`, not field presence. An unrecognized `kind` is SAFE-STOP: report it, issue no edit, never infer from fields.

- Act on `decision.kind` in `read.task_pack`: `act.answer`/`act.edit` closes discovery; use served evidence now. Batch independent changes in exactly one `edit_file` call via `edits[]` (each own `path`/`handle`). `discover` means execute its `next`; `await_input` means execute a carried `next`, otherwise re-pack once with candidate `targets`; `done` stops.
- A `refusal`'s `retry` is the only valid transition. Execute a carried `next` verbatim as the whole call; without one, obey `retry` and never invent a continuation. hints are advisory, not calls.
- A `read.receipt` is never a re-pack cue: follow `next` when present; otherwise act or stop on its restated evidence/certificate.
- Zoom the same handle when bytes are partial: request its `remaining` ranges (bounded). Follow `next` after a capped response; don't retry `full` or re-pack with a new query. Any read takes `budget.bytes`/`budget.tokens` to cap a response.
- Search uses `action:"find",queries:["<one identifier>"]`; several identifiers go in one `queries=[…]` call, never serial. Use `references` for call sites and `tree` for inventory.
- Name path+identifier+change plainly for a fast known single-value edit. Otherwise edit through a TL handle with `allowPathFallback:false`; new files use `create:true` and an explicit `cwd`. Under a live `act.edit` fence edit only files TL served this epoch (an unserved target refuses `execution-typestate` — run its `next`); `target:"all"` replaces every exact match (`applied[].replaced`); `create:true` items ride any batch once `cwd` pins them. The create response's `sha`, `bytes`, and `total_lines` prove it; skip the read-back. `operation_id` makes retries safe, flagging replays.
- After edits, run the returned verification kit before the first compile/test command. Its `code` is served evidence; `omitted` means read named `targets` in one batch via `next`; `verification.status:"not-applicable"` means no test/mock references the edit. Finish with tests or `git diff`, and stop once checks pass.
- Flag-gated, default OFF: TL_CWD_NEAR_MISS may auto-adopt a sibling `cwd` for `read_file`/`search_files`, disclosed via `cwd_corrected.reason` (`separator-class|sibling-edit-distance|prefix-truncation|segment-duplication`) — `edit_file` never auto-resolves, use `cwd_candidates`/`did_you_mean` on refusal. TL_SEARCH_DEDUP tags an identical repeat search `receipt.tag:"query-unchanged"` (still complete; `task.force_serve:true` bypasses). TL_RECEIPT_COVERAGE may add held covered_by spans to a receipt/partial read. TL_BATCH_HINTS hints after your 2nd serial slice of one file (optional ledger input declares held spans). SF v2 flags (default OFF) may ride a demoted/structural-fill candidate as a bodyless `evidence[]` row with `remaining`+`handle`, gate closure on `remaining`/`gaps` until a run TL cannot itself observe discharges them, expose a relation via `plan.wiring.evidence_graph`: definition (+callers/callees iff not `unavailable`), and anchor the frontier on a qualified Class::method.

Everything else — wiring, archives, credentials, and deep per-kind fields — arrives on demand in the response that needs it.
